{
  "rules": [
    "Regime-change / mean-reversion miss: forecasts stay anchored near ~70–73 even when the market transitions into a sustained downtrend; apply a negative drift adjustment when recent returns turn negative for several consecutive days.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–10) is slightly low during the final leg of an upswing, but medium/long horizon (days ~15–30) becomes strongly high-biased as the realized path mean-reverts and sells off; increase mean-reversion strength with horizon.",
    "Overshoot after local highs: after a run-up to ~72–73, the model extrapolates elevated levels instead of anticipating pullbacks; cap forward levels relative to recent peak and force partial retracement (e.g., fade a portion of the last 5–10 day rise).",
    "Lag in turning points: once the true series starts falling, forecasts decline too slowly and remain too smooth; add a momentum-triggered slope change (steepen downward path) when price breaks below a short moving average / recent support.",
    "Volatility underestimation on the downside: realized outcomes show sharper late-horizon drops (to low/mid-60s) than forecasts; widen downside scenarios or apply asymmetric error correction (larger adjustments for negative shocks than positive)."
  ]
}
